WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.414
The strongest path-traversal prevention
often starts at the API design.

2
00:00:04.494 --> 00:00:08.219
Instead of accepting a filesystem path,
accept an object identifier.

3
00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:11.519
An attachment endpoint can receive an
opaque attachment ID,

4
00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:13.052
load its storage metadata,

5
00:00:13.132 --> 00:00:16.351
and map that record to an
application-controlled location.

6
00:00:16.431 --> 00:00:18.704
The client identifies the object it wants;

7
00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:21.057
the server decides where the object lives.

8
00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:24.610
This removes most separators, parent
components, drive syntax,

9
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.152
and directory layout from the trust
boundary.

10
00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:29.884
Storage metadata should carry the root
category,

11
00:00:29.964 --> 00:00:32.994
server-generated relative name, tenant
relationship,

12
00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:34.968
and state needed for the operation.

13
00:00:35.048 --> 00:00:38.710
The application chooses from fixed roots
for attachments, exports,

14
00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.010
or temporary objects and resolves only
metadata it created.

15
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.320
Do not store arbitrary client paths in a
database and later treat the database as

16
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.284
proof of safety.

17
00:00:47.364 --> 00:00:49.574
Data can remain untrusted after storage,

18
00:00:49.654 --> 00:00:53.505
especially when it originated in imports,
old code, or another service.

19
00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:57.752
Validate the mapping when it is created
and again at the filesystem boundary as

20
00:00:57.832 --> 00:00:58.590
appropriate.

21
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.006
Opaque IDs and UUIDs are not
authorization.

22
00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:05.190
A valid record may belong to another user
or tenant, be quarantined, expired,

23
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.480
or unavailable for the requested action.

24
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.853
Load the record through trusted subject
and tenant scope, enforce object state,

25
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.069
then derive the path.

26
00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:17.379
Avoid returning raw storage locations in
API responses or using path secrecy as

27
00:01:17.459 --> 00:01:18.343
access control.

28
00:01:18.423 --> 00:01:22.022
Separate the external display name from
the internal storage name.

29
00:01:22.102 --> 00:01:23.744
Uploads follow the same model.

30
00:01:23.824 --> 00:01:28.369
Treat the original filename as untrusted
metadata for display when the product needs

31
00:01:28.449 --> 00:01:31.733
it, with appropriate encoding at every
later output context.

32
00:01:31.813 --> 00:01:35.916
Generate the stored filename on the
server, select a controlled upload root,

33
00:01:35.996 --> 00:01:38.648
and keep temporary and final locations
explicit.

34
00:01:38.728 --> 00:01:42.201
The extension and content also require
file-upload validation,

35
00:01:42.281 --> 00:01:44.869
but those checks do not authorize a
destination.

36
00:01:44.949 --> 00:01:48.232
By reducing the request to an object or
operation identifier,

37
00:01:48.312 --> 00:01:52.669
the service exposes a narrow storage
capability rather than a general filesystem

38
00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:53.380
interface.
